# Mountebank Studio — optional configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (git-ignored by the `*.local` rule) and edit. Everything
# here is optional: with no env file at all the panel starts empty and you add
# your instances in Settings, which is the normal path.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VITE_ENVIRONMENTS — pre-provision the environment list
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A JSON array, read ONCE on a fresh install to seed the list. After that the
# list lives in the browser (localStorage key `mountebank-studio-environments`)
# and the user's own edits win — changing this variable will not overwrite them.
# Anything malformed is skipped rather than crashing the app, so a typo here
# cannot lock anyone out.
#
# Per entry:
#   label     required. The name shown everywhere in the UI.
#   target    required. Absolute http(s) URL of the Mountebank admin API,
#             no trailing slash. This is the port `mb start` listens on
#             (2525 by default), not the port an imposter answers on.
#   id        optional. The first segment of every route, so links are stable.
#             Defaults to a slug of the label.
#   note      optional. A caution shown next to the environment.
#
# Worked example — one throwaway instance on the developer's own machine and one
# shared instance everybody reads. Must be one line:
#
# VITE_ENVIRONMENTS=[{"id":"local","label":"Local","target":"http://localhost:2525"},{"id":"shared","label":"Shared sandbox","target":"https://mb.example.com","note":"Other people depend on these mocks."}]
#
# Remember the other half: each of those instances has to have been started with
# this panel's origin allowed, e.g.
#
#   mb start --origin "http://localhost:5273"
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MB_PROXY — reach an instance through this origin instead
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# For an instance you cannot restart — one your DevOps team installed — the flag
# above is not yours to add. Forward it through the dev server instead: nothing is
# cross-origin then, so no flag, restart or permission is involved anywhere.
#
#   MB_PROXY=stage=https://mb.stg.example.com,dev=https://mb.dev.example.com
#
# That serves /mb/stage and /mb/dev, and those are what you enter as the admin API
# in the panel. The dev server also publishes the map at /mb/targets.json, which is
# how the panel can offer to switch an environment over without guessing which path
# belongs to which instance.
#
# No VITE_ prefix on purpose: this configures the dev server and must never be
# readable from client code. deploy/nginx.conf does the same job in production.
